WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.280
So we've talked about scoped.

00:02.280 --> 00:04.680
What about CSS modules?

00:04.680 --> 00:06.160
You might not have heard a lot about it,

00:06.160 --> 00:07.800
but, and this is a feature that's not

00:07.800 --> 00:10.320
talked about a lot about, but what you can do

00:10.320 --> 00:14.480
is you can actually go ahead and tell your module,

00:14.480 --> 00:18.840
or your CSS styles, to take on the module mode.

00:18.840 --> 00:21.120
Now, what this will do, though, is a little bit tricky,

00:21.120 --> 00:22.200
though, on the other hand.

00:22.200 --> 00:24.920
So let me show you what's happening.

00:24.920 --> 00:27.120
So right now, we'll notice that nothing's happening

00:27.120 --> 00:29.400
to the button right now, because CSS modules

00:29.400 --> 00:32.080
works really well in view, or I think, honestly,

00:32.080 --> 00:33.960
I think in JavaScript scenarios, when there's

00:33.960 --> 00:35.960
an actual class associated with it.

00:35.960 --> 00:38.240
So just to switch this up to show that it's equivalent,

00:38.240 --> 00:40.040
let me start by reverting this real quick.

00:40.040 --> 00:42.240
So we'll use a class of button, rather than just

00:42.240 --> 00:43.880
a generic button selector.

00:43.880 --> 00:47.720
And we'll show that this, on the button count, the base

00:47.720 --> 00:50.280
counter, let's make sure everything is equivalent.

00:50.280 --> 00:53.400
So all the buttons here, one, two, three,

00:53.400 --> 00:55.520
are going to get a class of button.

00:55.520 --> 00:56.240
So there we go.

00:56.240 --> 00:58.320
There's that red showing up, as we would expect.

00:58.320 --> 01:01.680
And on the user card, we get a class of button.

01:01.680 --> 01:02.200
All right.

01:02.200 --> 01:03.280
So now everything is equal.

01:03.280 --> 01:06.000
We're talking about CSS classes here now.

01:06.000 --> 01:07.280
OK.

01:07.280 --> 01:11.200
When we add the module here, what this is going to do

01:11.200 --> 01:13.200
is this is going to allow us to do something that might seem

01:13.200 --> 01:16.200
a little weird at first, but trust me on this one.

01:16.200 --> 01:19.460
What we're going to do is we're going to vbind our class

01:19.460 --> 01:20.880
attribute.

01:20.880 --> 01:22.800
And so I'm going to do the shorthand now.

01:22.800 --> 01:24.840
And rather than just bind it to button directly,

01:24.840 --> 01:26.200
because right now it'll be like, what do you mean button?

01:26.200 --> 01:28.080
There's no variable called button.

01:28.080 --> 01:32.240
We're going to do dollar sign style.button.

01:32.240 --> 01:34.600
That's what we're going to do.

01:34.600 --> 01:38.080
And so what you'll notice here, actually, I

01:38.080 --> 01:39.600
realized I only updated in one place.

01:39.600 --> 01:41.600
We didn't update it in all three.

01:41.600 --> 01:44.760
So one, two, three, just like that.

01:44.760 --> 01:47.040
OK.

01:47.040 --> 01:49.480
So you might at first think, oh, that looks kind of ugly.

01:49.480 --> 01:51.280
I don't like the JavaScript.

01:51.280 --> 01:52.800
But let me show you what's actually

01:52.800 --> 01:55.920
happening underneath the hood.

01:55.960 --> 01:58.280
If you take a look here, what you'll notice

01:58.280 --> 01:59.680
is that the class has actually now

01:59.680 --> 02:04.160
been recompiled to actually have a unique hash at the end.

02:04.160 --> 02:08.160
And this is really powerful, because what you end up getting

02:08.160 --> 02:11.600
is the ability to actually truly scope your styles

02:11.600 --> 02:14.120
to basically that particular component.

02:14.120 --> 02:16.760
Because the odds of this getting overridden by something else

02:16.760 --> 02:19.640
randomly is very, very low.

02:19.640 --> 02:22.720
And if you want it to be even more specific,

02:22.720 --> 02:24.640
there are actually ways to configure your compiler

02:24.680 --> 02:27.480
to actually basically spit out a specific pattern of how

02:27.480 --> 02:29.440
you want the modules to be spit out.

02:29.440 --> 02:30.840
So in this case, for example, you'll

02:30.840 --> 02:34.880
notice it tells you that the class of button is being used.

02:34.880 --> 02:37.040
So that way you can at least search for a class button.

02:37.040 --> 02:39.480
And then after that, of course, this is a randomly generated

02:39.480 --> 02:40.680
hash.

02:40.680 --> 02:44.680
At the bare minimum, this is to me the minimum style of DX

02:44.680 --> 02:46.800
that I think I recommend when it comes to CSS modules

02:46.800 --> 02:47.560
across frameworks.

02:47.560 --> 02:50.120
It's because I've noticed that some CSS modules will actually

02:50.120 --> 02:52.080
just hash the entire class.

02:52.080 --> 02:54.120
And you could argue, oh, that's super unique.

02:54.120 --> 02:56.840
But I'd also argue that is super hard to debug.

02:56.840 --> 02:59.320
In fact, that's almost near difficult and possible to debug

02:59.320 --> 03:01.640
because you're like, now I have to figure out the HTML where

03:01.640 --> 03:03.080
that happens to apply.

03:03.080 --> 03:04.760
And so what I typically recommend

03:04.760 --> 03:07.200
when it comes to CSS modules is to also go ahead

03:07.200 --> 03:09.040
and prefix the component name to it.

03:09.040 --> 03:11.000
Now, granted, if your component names are super long,

03:11.000 --> 03:13.200
then there might end up being a performance issue.

03:13.200 --> 03:15.480
But that way you know exactly which component file to drop

03:15.480 --> 03:17.240
into and where the CSS class is.

03:17.240 --> 03:20.360
But this is a really great way to protect your CSS

03:20.360 --> 03:21.640
for components.

03:21.680 --> 03:24.200
Now, once again, I know that not everyone is in love

03:24.200 --> 03:25.680
with this style syntax.

03:25.680 --> 03:27.160
So what I tell people is this.

03:27.160 --> 03:29.160
Remember that when you're using stuff in Vue,

03:29.160 --> 03:31.080
you can do stuff where it makes sense.

03:31.080 --> 03:32.680
Just because you want to use CSS modules

03:32.680 --> 03:34.680
doesn't mean you have to CSS modularize your entire code

03:34.680 --> 03:35.240
base.

03:35.240 --> 03:37.640
You can just do it in the places where it makes sense.

03:37.640 --> 03:39.960
And that way you don't feel like you have to, one, do it

03:39.960 --> 03:41.280
across the entire code base.

03:41.280 --> 03:43.400
But two, you can be selective about the techniques

03:43.400 --> 03:45.640
that you use because, as we saw earlier,

03:45.640 --> 03:48.800
scoped works really well when it's written well.

03:48.800 --> 03:51.620
But then otherwise, in times you need to be very protective,

03:51.660 --> 03:54.020
go ahead and try out CSS modules on it.

03:54.020 --> 03:55.700
And again, this is something that's just built right

03:55.700 --> 03:56.460
into the library.

03:56.460 --> 04:00.820
So the moment you want to use it, it's there for you.

04:00.820 --> 04:02.660
We won't show you this at this point,

04:02.660 --> 04:04.580
because this will be additional configuration.

04:04.580 --> 04:07.300
But just know that for these blocks as well,

04:07.300 --> 04:09.020
a lot of us also use preprocessors

04:09.020 --> 04:11.100
when it comes to using our styles.

04:11.100 --> 04:13.140
And so what you should know is that you can actually

04:13.140 --> 04:16.460
add attributes on here, like Lang CSS, for example.

04:16.460 --> 04:19.220
And you'll need to actually configure your builder

04:19.220 --> 04:21.060
to actually make sure it has the sass loader.

04:21.100 --> 04:23.860
But once you do that, you can write your sass files in here,

04:23.860 --> 04:26.340
just like you normally would, use all your sass syntax

04:26.340 --> 04:27.460
as you would expect.

04:27.460 --> 04:28.460
Question?

04:28.460 --> 04:32.380
Is there a way to avoid the class bind style button?

04:32.380 --> 04:36.580
Is there a way to use sass and bind to the parent element

04:36.580 --> 04:42.740
in the component, and then sass?

04:42.740 --> 04:44.340
Yeah, that's a great question.

04:44.340 --> 04:46.860
So the question is around trying to,

04:46.860 --> 04:50.220
instead of using the style to use sass to check out

04:50.220 --> 04:52.460
what the class is of the parent, and use that to generate

04:52.460 --> 04:54.900
the CSS, to wrap, and that's everything,

04:54.900 --> 04:58.060
I've never actually done that particular approach.

04:58.060 --> 05:00.460
I would imagine that if you were to approach that,

05:00.460 --> 05:02.140
it might be kind of hard to do.

05:02.140 --> 05:06.540
So I don't have a, I'll say that it sounds possible,

05:06.540 --> 05:07.980
but it might be tricky to do.

